      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <LifecycleNotice
    type="active"
    explanation="Cash Basis IPSAS (revised 2017) prescribes general purpose financial reporting under the cash basis of accounting. Part 1 sets mandatory requirements for recognizing cash receipts, payments, balances, and public budget comparisons; Part 2 identifies encouraged good practices to prepare entities for transition to accrual IPSAS."
    link=""
    linkText=""
  />

  <!-- ============ SECTION 1 — OBJECTIVE, STRUCTURE & THE TWO-PART FRAMEWORK ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Objective, Structure &amp; the Two-Part Framework</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Objective</div>
      <div class="principle">Prescribes the manner in which general purpose financial statements are presented under the CASH basis of accounting. Information about cash receipts, payments, and balances is necessary for accountability, and helps users assess an entity's ability to generate future cash and its likely sources/uses.</div>
      <div class="text-supporting" style="margin-top:8px;">Compliance enhances comprehensive, transparent reporting AND comparability — both with the entity's own prior periods, and with other cash-basis adopters.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Two parts, two different obligations</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.6fr 2.4fr;"><span>Part</span><span>Nature</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.6fr 2.4fr;"><span>PART 1</span><span>MANDATORY. Applies to all public sector entities preparing GPFS under the cash basis. Defines the cash basis, establishes disclosure requirements, addresses specific reporting issues. Must be FULLY complied with to claim conformity.</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.6fr 2.4fr;"><span>PART 2</span><span>NOT mandatory. Identifies ADDITIONAL accounting policies/disclosures an entity is ENCOURAGED to adopt — enhancing usefulness for accountability/decision-making, and supporting transition to accrual reporting.</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 — THE 2017 REVISION: WHAT CHANGED AND WHY ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>The 2017 Revision — What Changed and Why</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A brief history</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span>First issued January 2003 (effective periods from 1 January 2004)</span>
        <span>Updated in 2006/2007 with budget-information and external-assistance requirements (effective periods from 1 January 2009)</span>
        <span>REVISED in 2017 — the version this poster covers, superseding the 2007 Standard</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Three deliberate 2017 objectives</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span>REMOVE adoption obstacles — recast the FORMERLY MANDATORY requirements for consolidated financial statements, external assistance disclosure, and third-party payment disclosure as ENCOURAGEMENTS in Part 2 instead</span>
        <span>Ensure requirements/encouragements are NOT CONTRARY to the equivalent accrual IPSAS, except where cash-basis differences are genuinely appropriate</span>
        <span>CLARIFY the Standard's intended role — primarily a STEP on the path to accrual IPSAS adoption, not an end in itself</span>
      </div>
      <div class="note">This last point directly echoes the "Role of the Cash Basis IPSAS" framing throughout the document — see &sect;23.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 — SCOPE OF THE REQUIREMENTS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Scope of the Requirements (paras. 1.1.1–1.1.6)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">The three-part public sector entity test</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span>Responsible for delivering SERVICES to benefit the public and/or redistribute income and wealth</span>
        <span>Mainly financed by TAXES/TRANSFERS from other government levels, social contributions, debt, or fees</span>
        <span>Does NOT have a primary objective to make PROFITS</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The all-or-nothing compliance rule</div>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <div class="f-eq">Financial statements shall NOT be described as complying with this Standard UNLESS they comply with ALL the requirements in Part 1</div>
      </div>
      <div class="text-supporting">General purpose financial statements serve service recipients/resource providers who cannot demand custom reports — citizens, taxpayers, legislators, donors, lenders (para. 1.1.3). Applies equally to individual entities and to a reporting entity comprising a controlling entity plus controlled entities (para. 1.1.5).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 — DEFINITIONS: THE CASH BASIS & CASH EQUIVALENTS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Definitions — the Cash Basis &amp; Cash Equivalents (paras. 1.2.1–1.2.5)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Core vocabulary</div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Cash basis</div><div class="glossary-def">A basis of accounting that recognizes transactions and other events ONLY when cash is received or paid.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Cash</div><div class="glossary-def">Cash on hand, demand deposits, and cash equivalents.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Cash equivalents</div><div class="glossary-def">Short-term, highly liquid investments readily convertible to known cash amounts, subject to insignificant value-change risk.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The measurement focus, and what qualifies as a cash equivalent</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Measurement focus = cash balances AND changes therein. Notes MAY provide additional information about liabilities (payables, borrowings) and non-cash assets (receivables, investments, PP&amp;E) — but these never enter the primary statement (para. 1.2.2).</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span>Normally qualifies only with a SHORT maturity — typically three months or less from acquisition (para. 1.2.3)</span>
        <span>EQUITY investments are excluded UNLESS, in substance, they ARE cash equivalents</span>
        <span>Bank OVERDRAFTS repayable on demand may be included as a cash component, where integral to cash management (para. 1.2.4)</span>
        <span>Movements BETWEEN cash components are EXCLUDED from cash flows — that's cash management, not a genuine increase/decrease (para. 1.2.5)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 — CASH CONTROLLED BY THE REPORTING ENTITY ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Cash Controlled by the Reporting Entity — the Treasury Single Account (paras. 1.2.6–1.2.10)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">The control test</div>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <div class="f-eq">Cash is controlled when the entity can USE it for its own objectives (or otherwise benefit), and can EXCLUDE or REGULATE others' access to that benefit</div>
      </div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Where an entity collects cash on behalf of its government, or holds cash it will transfer to third parties, it controls that cash ONLY for the period it resides in its own bank account — before transfer onward (para. 1.2.7).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The "treasury single account" — a genuinely distinctive public sector mechanic</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Some governments manage department expenditure through a CENTRALIZED treasury function — a "treasury single account" — where individual departments don't hold their own separate bank accounts. The centralized function acts AS A BANK on behalf of departments; from ITS perspective, payments on their behalf are treated as changes WITHIN their accounts — mirroring how a commercial bank accounts for customer payments (para. 1.2.8). Where the CENTRAL entity itself controls the account(s), the transfers/payments through them ARE that central entity's own cash flows, administered on behalf of the individual departments (para. 1.2.9).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 — FINANCIAL STATEMENTS: REQUIRED COMPONENTS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Financial Statements — Required Components (paras. 1.3.4–1.3.11)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">The mandatory three (para. 1.3.4)</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span>A STATEMENT OF CASH RECEIPTS AND PAYMENTS recognizing ALL cash receipts, payments, and balances controlled by the entity</span>
        <span>ACCOUNTING POLICIES and explanatory notes</span>
        <span>WHEN the entity publishes an approved budget: a comparison of budget and actual amounts — either as a separate statement, or as budget columns within the statement of cash receipts and payments</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Optional additions, and the information/statement distinction</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Entities MAY include additional statements — e.g. cash by major fund category, borrowing sources/uses, or budget comparisons (para. 1.3.9). Entities commonly collect information the cash basis doesn't recognize — receivables, payables, borrowings, non-cash assets, commitments, contingent liabilities, performance indicators — and MAY disclose this in the NOTES where useful, without it entering the primary statements (paras. 1.3.10–1.3.11).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 — THE STATEMENT OF CASH RECEIPTS AND PAYMENTS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Information to be Presented in the Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments (paras. 1.3.12–1.3.17)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Required content (para. 1.3.12)</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span>TOTAL cash receipts, with a SUB-CLASSIFICATION appropriate to the entity's operations</span>
        <span>TOTAL cash payments, similarly sub-classified</span>
        <span>BEGINNING and CLOSING cash balances</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Gross reporting, with two net exceptions</div>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <div class="f-eq">Report on a GROSS basis, EXCEPT: (a) transactions the entity ADMINISTERS on behalf of others, recognized in the statement, or (b) items with QUICK turnover, LARGE amounts, and SHORT maturities</div>
      </div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Sub-classification is a matter of PROFESSIONAL JUDGMENT, applied in the context of the objectives and qualitative characteristics of cash-basis reporting (para. 1.3.17). Line items/headings/subtotals are added whenever necessary for FAIR PRESENTATION (para. 1.3.14).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 — ACCOUNTING POLICIES & EXPLANATORY NOTES ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Accounting Policies &amp; Explanatory Notes (paras. 1.3.25–1.3.33)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">The six qualitative characteristics (para. 1.3.27)</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span>UNDERSTANDABLE</span>
        <span>RELEVANT to decision-making/accountability needs</span>
        <span>FAITHFULLY REPRESENTATIVE — complete, neutral, free from material error</span>
        <span>COMPARABLE</span>
        <span>TIMELY</span>
        <span>VERIFIABLE</span>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Constraints: materiality, cost-benefit assessment, and an appropriate BALANCE across the six characteristics.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Structure, and the disclosure-doesn't-fix-bad-accounting rule</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span>Present the BASIS of preparation and specific policies for significant transactions</span>
        <span>Each face-of-statement item CROSS-REFERENCED to related note information (para. 1.3.26)</span>
      </div>
      <div class="callout danger callout--flush">INAPPROPRIATE accounting treatments are NEVER rectified by disclosing the policy used, or by notes/explanatory material (para. 1.3.30) — a direct parallel to IPSAS 1's own principle for accrual-basis reporting.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 — REPORTING PERIOD, TIMELINESS & AUTHORIZATION DATE ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Reporting Period, Timeliness &amp; Authorization Date (paras. 1.4.1–1.4.8)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Timeliness — a genuinely concrete benchmark</div>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <div class="f-eq">Issue within SIX MONTHS of the reporting date — a THREE-MONTH timeframe is STRONGLY encouraged</div>
      </div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Operational complexity is explicitly NOT a sufficient excuse for late reporting (para. 1.4.4) — a more concrete numerical benchmark than most Standards in this library provide.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Authorization date &amp; entity information</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span>Disclose the AUTHORIZATION DATE, and WHO gave that authorization — plus whether another body can AMEND the statements afterward (Ministers, government, Parliament) (paras. 1.4.5–1.4.6)</span>
        <span>Domicile, legal form, jurisdiction(s); nature of operations; governing legislation; significant entities/sectors presented, and CHANGES therein (para. 1.4.7)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 — RESTRICTIONS ON CASH BALANCES ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Restrictions on Cash Balances &amp; Access to Borrowings (paras. 1.4.9–1.4.12)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Three required disclosures (para. 1.4.9)</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span>Significant cash balances NOT AVAILABLE for use by the entity (e.g. exchange controls on a controlled entity abroad)</span>
        <span>Significant cash balances SUBJECT TO external restrictions (e.g. a grant/donation for a specific purpose, or agent-held cash not yet transferred)</span>
        <span>UNDRAWN BORROWING FACILITIES potentially available for future operating activities/capital commitments, with any usage restrictions</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Why this matters — a genuine liquidity signal</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Undrawn facilities represent a POTENTIAL source of future cash — disclosure lets readers assess availability AND the extent to which the entity has already drawn on them (para. 1.4.12). Restricted balances are still CONTROLLED (and reported as such), but separate disclosure genuinely helps readers understand what's actually free to use (para. 1.4.11).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 — CONSISTENCY, COMPARATIVE INFORMATION & FOREIGN CURRENCY ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Consistency, Error Correction &amp; Foreign Currency (paras. 1.4.13–1.4.19, 1.5.1–1.6.8)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Consistency, and correcting prior-period errors</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Presentation/classification stays consistent period to period UNLESS a significant operational change, or a future Standard amendment, justifies otherwise (para. 1.4.13). Prior-period errors are corrected by adjusting the OPENING cash balance, with comparative information RESTATED unless impracticable — disclosing the error's nature, correction amount, and restatement status (paras. 1.5.1–1.5.2).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Foreign currency — spot rate for transactions, closing rate for balances</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span>Foreign currency RECEIPTS/PAYMENTS &rarr; translated at the SPOT rate on the transaction date (para. 1.6.2)</span>
        <span>Foreign currency BALANCES &rarr; translated at the CLOSING rate (para. 1.6.3)</span>
        <span>A foreign controlled entity's cash flows &rarr; translated at rates on the DATES of the receipts/payments (para. 1.6.4)</span>
        <span>Exchange DIFFERENCES reconciling opening/closing balances are disclosed separately — they are NOT cash receipts/payments themselves (paras. 1.6.5, 1.6.8)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 — BUDGET & ACTUAL: DEFINITIONS & SCOPE ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Budget &amp; Actual — Definitions &amp; Scope (paras. 1.7.1–1.7.10)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Core vocabulary</div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Approved budget</div><div class="glossary-def">An expenditure authority derived from laws, appropriation bills, government ordinances, and other decisions related to anticipated revenue/receipts for the budget period.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Original budget</div><div class="glossary-def">The initially approved budget for the budget period — may include automatically-carried-forward appropriated amounts.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Final budget</div><div class="glossary-def">The original budget plus all authorized changes (supplemental appropriations, transfers between budget heads, funding cutbacks) applicable during the budget period.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Scope — applies wherever budgets are PUBLIC</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Applies to ALL entities that are required to, OR ELECT to, make their approved budget(s) publicly available. The Standard does NOT require budgets to be published — but once they ARE, the comparison requirement attaches (paras. 1.7.9–1.7.10). Whichever body has authority to withdraw funds from the treasury for identified purposes creates the "approved budget" — whether or not it becomes law (para. 1.7.3).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 13 — PRESENTATION OF THE BUDGET COMPARISON ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Presentation of a Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts (paras. 1.7.8, 1.7.17–1.7.20)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Three required elements, per legislative oversight level (para. 1.7.8)</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span>ORIGINAL and FINAL budget amounts</span>
        <span>ACTUAL amounts on a COMPARABLE basis</span>
        <span>A note EXPLANATION of material differences (or a cross-reference to other public documents containing it)</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Two presentation routes — comparable basis is the gatekeeper</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Basis alignment</span><span>Presentation route</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Budget IS comparable (same accounting basis, entity, period, classification)</span><span>Additional budget COLUMNS within the statement of cash receipts and payments (para. 1.7.17)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Budget is NOT comparable</span><span>A SEPARATE "statement of comparison of budget and actual amounts" (para. 1.7.19)</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Aggregation level follows the budget documents' OWN broad classes/heads as approved by the legislature — ensuring the comparison genuinely reflects what was actually authorized (para. 1.7.20).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 14 — RECONCILIATION OF ACTUAL AMOUNTS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Reconciliation of Actual Amounts on a Comparable Basis and Actual Amounts in the Financial Statements (paras. 1.7.41–1.7.46)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Three difference types (para. 1.7.42)</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span><strong>BUDGETARY BASIS differences</strong> — the budget uses a DIFFERENT accounting basis than the financial statements (e.g. accrual budget, cash statements)</span>
        <span><strong>TIMING differences</strong> — the budget period differs from the financial statements' reporting period</span>
        <span><strong>ENTITY differences</strong> — the budget omits programs/entities that ARE part of the reporting entity</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">When reconciliation is required — and when it isn't</div>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <div class="f-eq">Reconcile comparable-basis actual amounts to TOTAL cash receipts/payments, on the face of the comparison statement OR in the notes — required ONLY where the financial statements and budget are NOT on a comparable basis</div>
      </div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Where BOTH the budget and statements use the cash basis, for the SAME period/entities/format &rarr; NO reconciliation is needed (para. 1.7.44). Comparative-period reconciliation is explicitly NOT required (para. 1.7.45).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 15 — TRANSITIONAL PROVISIONS & EFFECTIVE DATE ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Effective Date of Part 1 &amp; Transitional Provisions (paras. 1.8.1–1.8.9)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A genuine grace period for existing adopters</div>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <div class="f-eq">First-time adopters apply ALL provisions from first adoption. EXISTING (2007 Standard) adopters are NOT required to comply until 1 JANUARY 2019 — up to a two-year transition window</div>
      </div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Entities using this relief must DISCLOSE which policies they haven't yet adopted (paras. 1.8.2–1.8.4).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Effective date &amp; withdrawal of the 2007 Standard</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span>Periods beginning on/after <strong>1 January 2019</strong> — earlier application encouraged, with disclosure if elected (para. 1.8.5)</span>
        <span>This Standard SUPERSEDES the 2007 Cash Basis IPSAS (para. 1.8.7)</span>
        <span>2007 Standard's own timeline: first issued Jan 2003 (effective 1 Jan 2004); budget/external-assistance additions effective 1 Jan 2009 (para. 1.8.8)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 16 — ILLUSTRATIVE STATEMENT FORMATS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Illustrative Statement Formats — Government A (Appendix 1A)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments (illustrative, condensed)</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>(in thousands of currency units)</span><span>200X</span><span>200X-1</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span><strong>RECEIPTS</strong> — Taxation (income, VAT, property, other)</span><span>X</span><span>X</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Donations, Grants and Other Aid</span><span>X</span><span>X</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Borrowings (commercial institutions, development banks)</span><span>X</span><span>X</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Capital Receipts (PP&amp;E and financial instrument disposals)</span><span>X</span><span>X</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Trading Activities &amp; Other receipts</span><span>X</span><span>X</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span><strong>Total receipts</strong></span><span><strong>X</strong></span><span><strong>X</strong></span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span><strong>PAYMENTS</strong> — Operations (wages, supplies)</span><span>(X)</span><span>(X)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Transfers (grants, other transfer payments)</span><span>(X)</span><span>(X)</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Capital Payments (PP&amp;E, financial instruments)</span><span>(X)</span><span>(X)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Loan and Interest Repayments</span><span>(X)</span><span>(X)</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Other payments</span><span>(X)</span><span>(X)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span><strong>Total payments</strong></span><span><strong>(X)</strong></span><span><strong>(X)</strong></span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Cash beginning of year &rarr; Cash at end of year</span><span>X &rarr; X</span><span>X &rarr; X</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Condensed from the Handbook's own illustration — the actual statement includes full sub-classification detail and note references.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid grid-spaced">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts (illustrative, condensed)</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr"><span>(in thousands of currency units)</span><span>Actual</span><span>Final Budget</span><span>Original Budget</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr"><span>Taxation, Grants/Aid, Borrowing proceeds, Trading, Other &rarr; <strong>Total receipts</strong></span><span>X</span><span>X</span><span>X</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr"><span>Health / Education / Public order / Social protection / Defense / Housing / Recreation / Economic affairs / Environment / General public services</span><span>(X)</span><span>(X)</span><span>(X)</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr"><span><strong>Total payments</strong></span><span>(X)</span><span>(X)</span><span>(X)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 0.85fr 0.85fr 0.85fr"><span><strong>NET CASH FLOWS</strong></span><span>X</span><span>X</span><span>X</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Payments classified by FUNCTION here — a COFOG-style breakdown. A "Difference: Final Budget and Actual" column is NOT required, but is permitted if the entity wishes to include it. Optional additional statements can further break down receipts by fund classification, or payments by program/activity/function.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 17 — PART 2 OVERVIEW: GOING CONCERN & ADMINISTERED TRANSACTIONS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Part 2 Overview — Going Concern &amp; Administered Transactions (paras. 2.1.3–2.1.13)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Going concern — genuinely different for government vs. individual entities</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Disclosure of material going-concern uncertainties is ENCOURAGED, primarily relevant to INDIVIDUAL entities rather than government as a whole. A government's TAXING power may sustain going-concern status even with cash payments exceeding receipts for extended periods — an assumption that simply doesn't apply the same way to an ordinary business (paras. 2.1.4–2.1.5).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Administered transactions — cash the entity handles but doesn't control</div>
      <div class="formula-box">
        <div class="f-eq">Encouraged: disclose the AMOUNT and NATURE of cash flows/balances from transactions administered AS AN AGENT for others, where outside the entity's own control</div>
      </div>
      <div class="text-supporting">If these flows DON'T pass through a controlled bank account, they're excluded from the primary statement entirely — but disclosure still adds value, showing the SCOPE of the entity's activities (para. 2.1.7). A worked example: a Department of Taxation controls its OWN appropriated operating cash, but collected TAX revenue passed straight to a government trust fund is NOT controlled by the Department — it's an administered transaction (paras. 2.1.9–2.1.10).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 18 — DISCLOSURE OF MAJOR CLASSES OF CASH FLOWS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s18"><span class="num">18</span><h2>Disclosure of Major Classes of Cash Flows — Nature vs. Function (paras. 2.1.14–2.1.21)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Method 1 — Nature of Payments</div>
      <div class="text-supporting" style="margin-bottom:8px;">Aggregates payments by their INTRINSIC nature — never reallocated across functions.</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Cash payments</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Wages and salaries</span><span>(X)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Transport costs</span><span>(X)</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Capital acquisitions</span><span>(X)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Borrowing costs</span><span>(X)</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Other</span><span>(X)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span><strong>Total payments</strong></span><span><strong>(X)</strong></span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Method 2 — Function of Payments</div>
      <div class="text-supporting" style="margin-bottom:8px;">Classifies by PROGRAM/PURPOSE — often more relevant to users, though allocation can require judgment.</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Cash payments</span><span></span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Health services</span><span>(X)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Education services</span><span>(X)</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Other</span><span>(X)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span><strong>Total payments</strong></span><span><strong>(X)</strong></span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top:6px;">Entities using this method are encouraged to ALSO disclose payment-nature detail (e.g. salaries) as supplementary information (para. 2.1.20). At least ONE of the two methods is encouraged.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 19 — CONSOLIDATED FINANCIAL STATEMENTS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s19"><span class="num">19</span><h2>Consolidated Financial Statements — Encouraged, Not Required (paras. 2.1.33–2.1.46)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">The reversal from 2007 — now purely an encouragement</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">A controlling entity is ENCOURAGED (not required) to present consolidated financial statements covering ALL controlled entities, foreign and domestic, applying full elimination of intra-economic-entity cash balances/transactions, adjusting for differing reporting dates, and using uniform accounting policies (para. 2.1.37). This is the single biggest change from the 2007 Standard — see &sect;2.</div>
      <div class="note">Where full consolidation isn't presented, the entity is STILL encouraged to consolidate at least the budget sector, general government sector, or another core-activity grouping that responds to user needs (para. 2.1.38).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A parallel exemption to IPSAS 35's own consolidation relief</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Consolidated statements are UNNECESSARY where the controlling entity: is itself controlled (with users' needs met by ITS controlling entity's statements, and other owners not objecting); has no publicly-traded debt/equity; isn't filing for public-market issuance; AND its own ultimate/intermediate controlling entity produces public consolidated statements complying with EITHER the Cash Basis IPSAS or the accrual IPSAS (para. 2.1.40) — deliberately mirroring IPSAS 35's own four-condition exemption test from the accrual-basis series.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 20 — JOINT ARRANGEMENTS & HYPERINFLATIONARY ECONOMIES ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s20"><span class="num">20</span><h2>Joint Arrangements &amp; Hyperinflationary Economies (paras. 2.1.62–2.1.72)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Joint arrangements — simple cash-basis treatment</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Disclosure is ENCOURAGED for fair presentation. On a cash basis, entities generally report: cash EXPENDED acquiring/operating a joint arrangement interest as a PAYMENT, and cash RECEIVED from it as a RECEIPT (para. 2.1.63) — far simpler than the equity-method or line-by-line mechanics IPSAS 36/37 require under accrual accounting.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Hyperinflation — five judgment indicators, no fixed threshold</div>
      <div class="dot-list">
        <span>Population prefers non-monetary assets or stable foreign currency</span>
        <span>Monetary amounts thought of in a stable foreign currency, not local currency</span>
        <span>Credit sales/purchases price in expected purchasing-power loss, even over short periods</span>
        <span>Interest/wages/prices are LINKED to a price index</span>
        <span>Cumulative three-year inflation approaches or EXCEEDS 100%</span>
      </div>
      <div class="note">No single absolute rate is specified — judgment governs (para. 2.1.65). Where hyperinflation is present, RESTATEMENT of the statement of cash receipts and payments (and comparatives) in current-measuring-unit terms is encouraged, using a general price index — with restatement AS the primary presentation, not a supplement (paras. 2.1.66–2.1.69). Related budgetary information is similarly encouraged for restatement (para. 2.1.71).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 21 — PAYMENTS BY THIRD PARTIES & EXTERNAL/OTHER ASSISTANCE ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s21"><span class="num">21</span><h2>Payments by Third Parties &amp; External/Other Assistance (paras. 2.1.77–2.1.90)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Third-party payments — benefit without cash control</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Where a third party directly settles the entity's obligations or purchases goods/services for its benefit (e.g. a national government paying a construction company directly to build a road for a provincial government), the recipient entity NEVER controls the cash — so it doesn't meet this Standard's own "cash" definition. Yet the recipient clearly BENEFITS. Disclosure of the total amount and sub-classification is encouraged, once the entity has been FORMALLY ADVISED or otherwise VERIFIED the payment occurred (paras. 2.1.77–2.1.79).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">External vs. other assistance — the key distinguishing line</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Term</span><span>Meaning</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>External assistance</span><span>OFFICIAL resources — loans/grants/technical assistance/guarantees from MULTILATERAL or BILATERAL agencies, or from ANOTHER nation's government, under a binding agreement</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Other assistance</span><span>Resources from NGOs, or VOLUNTARILY provided by individuals/private organizations — excludes taxes, fines, fees, exchange-transaction resources, and same-nation intergovernmental transfers</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Disclosure encouraged: total external assistance received in cash; total paid by third parties to settle the entity's obligations; and (where practicable) similar detail for other assistance (para. 2.1.90).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 22 — REQUIRED AND ENCOURAGED DISCLOSURES & THE IPSAS 33 LINK ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s22"><span class="num">22</span><h2>Required and Encouraged Disclosures &amp; the IPSAS 33 Link (paras. 2.2.7–2.2.9)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Alignment with accrual IPSAS — mostly, with deliberate extras</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">This Standard's requirements/encouragements are NOT INCONSISTENT with the equivalent accrual IPSAS — but in some areas (third-party payments, external/other assistance) it encourages disclosures the accrual IPSAS DON'T require at all. These extras exist because they're genuinely useful for accountability/decision-making, AND for the "special purpose" needs of assistance providers monitoring their own funding (para. 2.2.7).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The bridge to accrual adoption — cross-referencing IPSAS 33</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">IPSAS 33 provides first-time adopters up to THREE YEARS of transitional relief from certain accrual IPSAS requirements (see that Standard's own poster, &sect;5). Depending which exemptions are elected, an entity identifies its statements as either TRANSITIONAL IPSAS financial statements (fair-presentation-affecting exemptions used) or full accrual-IPSAS-COMPLIANT statements (only non-fair-presentation-affecting exemptions used) (paras. 2.2.8–2.2.9). This closes the loop this Introduction opened — the Cash Basis IPSAS exists PRECISELY to prepare entities for this eventual IPSAS 33 transition.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 23 — RELATIONSHIP TO ACCRUAL IPSAS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s23"><span class="num">23</span><h2>Relationship to Accrual IPSAS — No IFRS Equivalent Exists</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A genuinely IPSAS-only concept — no IAS/IFRS comparison in the Handbook</div>
      <div class="principle">Unlike almost every other Standard in this library, the Cash Basis IPSAS has NO "Comparison with IAS/IFRS" section at all — IFRS has never addressed cash-basis reporting, since it assumes accrual accounting throughout. Instead, this Standard's own internal "comparison" is with the ACCRUAL IPSAS body of literature (IPSAS 1–51) it is designed to bridge toward.</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1.9fr;"><span>Dimension</span><span>Relationship to accrual IPSAS</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1.9fr;"><span>Measurement focus</span><span>Cash Basis IPSAS: cash balances and CHANGES therein only. Accrual IPSAS: the full asset/liability/revenue/expense recognition framework across this entire library</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1.9fr;"><span>Consolidation</span><span>Cash Basis IPSAS: ENCOURAGED only (&sect;19), deliberately mirroring IPSAS 35's exemption test. Accrual IPSAS: generally REQUIRED subject to IPSAS 35's own exemptions</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1.9fr;"><span>Terminology consistency</span><span>Deliberately kept consistent where possible — "control," "economic entity," "controlling/controlled entity" all carry the SAME meaning as their IPSAS 35 counterparts</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1.9fr;"><span>Transition destination</span><span>IPSAS 33 governs the actual first-time accrual adoption process this Standard is designed to precede (&sect;22)</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 24 — STATEMENT / PROCESS MAPPING ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s24"><span class="num">24</span><h2>Statement / Process Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Master decision map</div>
      <ol class="ordered-list">
        <li>Does the entity meet the public sector test (service delivery, tax/transfer-financed, non-profit)? &rarr; Part 1 applies mandatorily to cash-basis GPFS (&sect;3)</li>
        <li>Prepare the THREE mandatory components: statement of cash receipts and payments, accounting policies/notes, and (if the budget is public) a budget comparison (&sect;6, &sect;12–&sect;14)</li>
        <li>Determine what CASH is actually CONTROLLED — apply the treasury-single-account and agent-transaction logic where relevant (&sect;5)</li>
        <li>Classify receipts/payments appropriately — gross, with the two narrow net exceptions (&sect;7); disclose by NATURE or FUNCTION, or both (&sect;18)</li>
        <li>Disclose restrictions, foreign currency treatment, authorization date, and entity information (&sect;9–&sect;11)</li>
        <li>Consider Part 2's ENCOURAGED disclosures — going concern, administered transactions, consolidated statements, joint arrangements, hyperinflation, third-party payments, external/other assistance (&sect;17–&sect;21)</li>
        <li>Plan the eventual TRANSITION — IPSAS 33's exemptions await once accrual adoption begins (&sect;22)</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid grid-spaced">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Concept-to-statement mapping table</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Cash Basis IPSAS concept</span><span>Statement(s)</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Total cash receipts/payments, sub-classified</span><span>Cash Receipts &amp; Payments</span><span>Mandatory, gross basis</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Beginning/closing cash balances</span><span>Cash Receipts &amp; Payments</span><span>Mandatory reconciling items</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Original/final budget vs. actual</span><span>Budget Comparison</span><span>Only if budget is publicly available</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Restrictions, authorization date, foreign currency</span><span><span class="badge be">Notes</span></span><span>Mandatory Part 1 disclosures</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Administered transactions, external assistance</span><span><span class="badge be">Notes</span></span><span>Encouraged Part 2 disclosures</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Consolidated economic entity statements</span><span>All statements</span><span>Encouraged, not required</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 25 — RELATED STANDARDS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s25"><span class="num">25</span><h2>Related Standards — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How the Cash Basis IPSAS connects across the broader IPSASB body of literature</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.9fr 2.0fr;"><span>Document</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.9fr 2.0fr;"><span>Preface to IPSAS</span><span>Governs how commentary paragraphs relate to authoritative "shall" requirements throughout this Standard (&sect;3)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.9fr 2.0fr;"><span>IPSAS 2</span><span>Conceptual parallel — the accrual-basis cash flow statement's own operating/investing/financing classification framework</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.9fr 2.0fr;"><span>IPSAS 4</span><span>Conceptual parallel for foreign currency translation mechanics (&sect;11)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.9fr 2.0fr;"><span>IPSAS 18</span><span>Conceptual parallel for functional/segment-style classification of cash flows (&sect;18)</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.9fr 2.0fr;"><span>IPSAS 24</span><span>Conceptual parallel — the accrual-basis budget-vs-actual comparison and reconciliation framework this Standard's own budget sections closely echo (&sect;12–&sect;14)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.9fr 2.0fr;"><span>IPSAS 33</span><span>Governs the actual TRANSITION to accrual IPSAS this Standard is designed to precede (&sect;22)</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.9fr 2.0fr;"><span>IPSAS 35</span><span>Source of the control definition and consolidation exemption test this Standard's own encouraged consolidation guidance deliberately mirrors (&sect;19)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.9fr 2.0fr;"><span>IPSAS 36 / 37</span><span>Conceptual parallel for joint arrangement interests, treated far more simply here on a cash basis (&sect;20)</span></div>
        <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.9fr 2.0fr;"><span>IPSAS 4 (Hyperinflation, formerly IPSAS 10)</span><span>Conceptual parallel for hyperinflationary economy restatement guidance (&sect;20)</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 26 — GLOSSARY ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s26"><span class="num">26</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="three-col">
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Cash basis</div><div class="glossary-def">A basis of accounting recognizing transactions and events only when cash is received or paid.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Cash equivalents</div><div class="glossary-def">Short-term, highly liquid investments readily convertible to known amounts of cash.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Approved budget</div><div class="glossary-def">Expenditure authority derived from laws, appropriation bills, ordinances, and related decisions for the budget period.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">External assistance</div><div class="glossary-def">Official resources the recipient can use or benefit from, provided by multilateral/bilateral agencies or another nation's government.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Economic entity</div><div class="glossary-def">A controlling entity and its controlled entities, for financial reporting purposes.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Treasury single account</div><div class="glossary-def">A centralized treasury function acting as a bank on behalf of individual government departments, which don't hold their own separate bank accounts.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="legend">
    <div class="ltitle">Financial statement key</div>
    <div class="lrow">
      <span class="badge bs">Statement</span> Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments
      <span class="badge bp">Budget</span> Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts
      <span class="badge be">Notes</span> Notes to the financial statements
    </div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
